The working of the video card is described here.

Unlike the main memory, the video memory does not have tag bits, so it
cannot contain capability values.

The screen consists of windows; a window that is mapped has a rectangular
area on the screen (or inside of another window) that it corresponds to,
that the contents of the window is displayed. Additionally, a window has
a frame program and pixel program, and several registers.

Some registers (the "protected registers") can only be set by the operating
system (from the CPU), while other registers will be writable by the frame
program as well. The protected registers are not readable either.

The protected registers can be:

* Position (relative to container) and size.

* Window zoom, which causes the contents of the window to appear bigger on
the screen. Only integer zooms are possible.

* Offset and length into video memory assigned to this window. It is
allowed to overlap other memory. All memory accesses by this window will
automatically apply the offset specified here and limit access to the
length specified here.

The unprotected registers can be:

* Tile width and tile height. The pixel coordinates within the window are
effectively division and modulo by these numbers and the results are used
as input into the pixel program; however, the implementation would be more
efficient than division and modulo.

* Tile X skip and tile Y skip. These are effectively multiplied by the X
and Y tile positions (although more efficiently which does not require an
actual multiplication) to use as inputs to the pixel program, rather than
using the X and Y coordinates directly.

* Fine X scroll and fine Y scroll. These are added to the window pixel
coordinates before applying the other calculations.

* General purpose registers. These are readable and writable by the frame
program, and are readable (but not writable) by the pixel program.

The frame program and pixel program involve different instruction sets and
are used for different purposes.

The frame program can read and write video memory (within the window's
memory block) and unprotected registers, and is executed once per frame;
this can be used to precalculate things especially those which will change
per frame or depend on things set by application programs.

The pixel program is executed for each pixel (possibly in parallel, in some
cases), and is much more limited than the frame program; it can only read
video memory and the unprotected general purpose registers, as well as the
inputs of the tile and pixel position. The pixel program does not have any
flow controls. The output of the pixel program is always 24-bits, with
8-bits per RGB channel, to decide the colour of this pixel.

In addition to windows, there are also sprites, which belong to a window
and can be displayed in that window as well as its subwindows, and overlaps
the contents of the window and subwindows. A sprite only has a position,
palette, and picture, so does not have all features of windows. (The mouse
cursor would use a sprite attached to the root window; some programs might
use additional sprites.)

The date/time display of the operating system would use its own window and
contains the appropriate fixpitch bitmap fonts for the language being used,
and then the numbers are displayed by the pixel program. Hopefully, this
would make it efficient even if updated every second.
